Custom Modules (part 1)
- modules are python files that contain statements, functions, classes, etc.
- module name is the file name without the .py
- modules are imported to other files using 'import'
- can import just parts of a module 'from (module) import (part)'
- many common modules (os, sys)
- shape nodes in maya must always be parented to a transform node to exist
- fundamental rule of DAG (directed acyclic graph) in maya
- camera/mesh/light in maya need a way to explain where they are placed in a scene (transform)
- this is accomplished by always parenting a shape node to the transform node
- the outliner can display shape nodes is (Display > Shapes) is true
- finding a nodes relatives and parents is common functionality

Custom Modules (part 2)
- maya creates a default directory for users
- two locations for saving scripts
    - main scripts folder allows your code to be used by any maya version
    - maya specific sub folder (2022/2023)
- in order for a module to be available in maya it must be saved on the python path
- the various script directories created by maya on on the python path
- important design practice when creating shelf button or menu items
    - encapsulate the functionality into its own module/function so the item is a single call
    - this ensures that any changes in the future will propagate without issue

Custom Modules (part 3)
- maya only loads script at startup
    - importing all required modules and ignoring future calls to import
- any scripting changes while a maya session is running will not get picked up
- to get around this we can use the importlib module to force maya to reload modules
    - import importlib
    - importlib.reload(module)
- this is only really required in the shelf or menu item code
- import lib is necessary evil when developing, but should be removed if releasing scripts for production
- *charcoal editor: alternative to import lib by removing current imports to force import calls to be triggered again
    - (Command > Python > Revert Module State)

